course: dictionary
lesson: 11546
title: Amenities: the useful things in a place
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Amenities are the useful or pleasant things and services in a place, such as parks, shops, toilets, a swimming pool or Wi-Fi. They make life easier or more enjoyable. The word is usually plural, «amenities», because a place has more than one.
explain: Estate agents use the word in adverts. «The flat is close to amenities, with a shop and a park just across the road.» The clues are shop and park. They are the useful things nearby, so amenities means useful things and services close by.
explain: Holiday reviews use it too. «The hotel had good amenities, including a pool and free Wi-Fi.» Ask yourself what would fit here. A pool and Wi-Fi are things that make a stay pleasant, so the meaning fits.
explain: In UK informal speech, «public amenities» can mean public toilets. Read the words around it: if the sentence is about toilets, the context tells you which meaning is used.
fact: Amenities means useful or pleasant things and services in a place.
fact: Examples of amenities are parks, shops, toilets, a swimming pool and Wi-Fi.
fact: The word is usually plural: one thing is an amenity, and more than one are amenities.
fact: «Local amenities» means the useful things in the area near where you live.
fact: In informal UK speech, «public amenities» can mean public toilets.
exercise: «The hotel had good amenities, including a pool and free Wi-Fi.» What are amenities here? | *Useful and pleasant things a place offers | Rules that guests must follow | Staff who clean the rooms | Rooms for sleeping only
exercise: Which of these is an amenity in a town? | A puddle on the road | A stone on the path | *A public library | A cloud in the sky
exercise: Which word is the singular of amenities? | amenit | amenities | amenitie | *amenity
test: «The campsite has basic amenities: clean toilets and hot showers.» What does basic amenities suggest here? | *The most important, everyday things a place needs | Rules about noise | Very expensive extras | Animals that live on the campsite
test: Mum says, «Public amenities are just down the road.» In UK informal speech, what might she mean? | A school for young children | *Public toilets | A bus timetable | A famous football stadium
exam: Which description fits amenities best? | A loud sound made by traffic | *Useful and pleasant things and services in a place, such as shops, parks and toilets | A type of weather | A person who runs a school
